==============================================================================
  Mutation tests  (assertion-strength validation)
==============================================================================

  Strategy: replace each named fact's body with `{}` (no constraint),
  then re-run Alloy. Each listed assertion should now FAIL on the
  mutated model. If it still PASSes, the assertion is vacuous and
  must be strengthened.

  - MUTATE F_AuthRequired  → runs/B-L1/mutated_F_AuthRequired.als
    rationale: removing F_AuthRequired lets the model invent a successful operation that was never authenticated, which is exactly the auth-bypass counterexample these assertions catch.
    expected to FAIL: AuthRequiredEverywhere, FR_001_AuthRequired
      - AuthRequiredEverywhere: FAIL (expected)
      - FR_001_AuthRequired: FAIL (expected)

  - MUTATE F_AppendOnlyDeletions  → runs/B-L1/mutated_F_AppendOnlyDeletions.als
    rationale: removing the uniqueness constraint on TaskDeletion.task allows two log rows to reference the same task, simulating an UPDATE/DELETE on the append-only deletion log.
    expected to FAIL: AppendOnly
      - AppendOnly: FAIL (expected)

  - MUTATE F_AuditCompleteness  → runs/B-L1/mutated_F_AuditCompleteness.als
    rationale: removing the completeness link allows a successful DeleteOp without a corresponding TaskDeletion row, which is the missing-audit-entry counterexample.
    expected to FAIL: AuditCompleteness, FR_018_DeletionLogged
      - AuditCompleteness: PASS (vacuous?)
      - FR_018_DeletionLogged: PASS (vacuous?)

  - MUTATE F_AttributionCorrectness  → runs/B-L1/mutated_F_AttributionCorrectness.als
    rationale: removing the attribution constraint lets a deletion row record a different user than the operation's actual caller, the misattribution counterexample.
    expected to FAIL: AttributionCorrectness
      - AttributionCorrectness: FAIL (expected)

  - MUTATE F_ClosedTaskRule  → runs/B-L1/mutated_F_ClosedTaskRule.als
    rationale: removing the closed-task rule lets the model invent a successful PATCH on a Done task that doesn't simultaneously reopen it — the exact 409 task_closed scenario the contract is built to forbid.
    expected to FAIL: FR_011_ClosedTaskEditRule
      - FR_011_ClosedTaskEditRule: FAIL (expected)

  - MUTATE F_NoOpOnDeletedTask  → runs/B-L1/mutated_F_NoOpOnDeletedTask.als
    rationale: removing the no-op-after-deletion rule allows a non-delete operation to target a task that has already been logged as deleted, breaking the 'deletion is permanent' guarantee.
    expected to FAIL: FR_012_NoOpAfterDeletion
      - FR_012_NoOpAfterDeletion: FAIL (expected)

